1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,780
Authentication establishes identity; authorization decides whether that identity may perform an operation

2
00:00:05,780 --> 00:00:07,225
on a resource.

3
00:00:07,375 --> 00:00:13,881
Validate token signatures, issuer, audience, and expiry using maintained security libraries.

4
00:00:14,031 --> 00:00:17,518
Do not merely decode a JWT and trust its claims.

5
00:00:17,668 --> 00:00:21,867
Role checks alone may be insufficient when ownership or tenant boundaries matter.

6
00:00:22,017 --> 00:00:26,047
Enforce authorization in the service that owns the data, even when a

7
00:00:26,047 --> 00:00:27,726
gateway already checked the request.

8
00:00:27,876 --> 00:00:33,835
Protect secrets, restrict CORS deliberately, validate input, and use TLS outside a

9
00:00:33,835 --> 00:00:34,829
local environment.

10
00:00:34,979 --> 00:00:40,297
CSRF concerns depend on how credentials are attached by browsers; disabling it

11
00:00:40,297 --> 00:00:43,399
without understanding the authentication model is unsafe.

12
00:00:43,549 --> 00:00:48,480
The supplied commerce services are explicitly local learning apps, bound to loopback,

13
00:00:48,480 --> 00:00:50,945
with synthetic data and no authentication.

14
00:00:51,095 --> 00:00:57,192
Before exposing them, add an OAuth2 resource-server configuration, account ownership, tenant-scoped idempotency,

15
00:00:57,192 --> 00:01:00,748
and tests for cross-account reads and writes.

16
00:01:00,898 --> 00:01:05,274
The AI track separately demonstrates trusted tenant checks in its tool boundary.

17
00:01:05,424 --> 00:01:10,351
Add a secured profile using a local identity provider or test JWT

18
00:01:10,351 --> 00:01:10,761
decoder.

19
00:01:10,911 --> 00:01:16,670
Test missing token, wrong issuer, wrong audience, expired token, insufficient role, and

20
00:01:16,670 --> 00:01:18,110
another user’s order.

21
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:21,509
Keep the default local demo limitations documented.

22
00:01:21,659 --> 00:01:26,215
Use Spring Security’s resource-server support and explicit authorization rules.

23
00:01:26,365 --> 00:01:30,935
Derive tenant identity from validated claims mapped by trusted configuration.

24
00:01:31,085 --> 00:01:34,232
Test resource ownership independently of role membership.

25
00:01:34,382 --> 00:01:38,089
Do not commit credentials or signing keys to the course repository.

26
00:01:38,239 --> 00:01:38,910
Question.

27
00:01:39,060 --> 00:01:42,511
What is the difference between 401 and 403?

28
00:01:42,661 --> 00:01:43,238
Answer.

29
00:01:43,388 --> 00:01:50,316
401 indicates missing or invalid authentication; 403 indicates the authenticated caller lacks

30
00:01:50,316 --> 00:01:53,780
permission, subject to deliberate resource-hiding policies.

31
00:01:53,930 --> 00:01:54,602
Question.

32
00:01:54,752 --> 00:01:57,459
Why is gateway-only authorization insufficient?

33
00:01:57,609 --> 00:01:58,186
Answer.

34
00:01:58,336 --> 00:02:03,806
Internal calls, misconfiguration, or alternate routes can bypass the gateway; the data-owning

35
00:02:03,806 --> 00:02:06,541
service must enforce its own policy.
